# Rendered by paisans. Do not edit: `paisans apply` overwrites this file.
#
# Routing for chat, imported by the gateway's block for
# chat.example.org. Two services answer on this one hostname: the homeserver and
# Matrix Authentication Service in front of it.
#
# WHICH HANDLE WINS IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is the thing to get right here.
# `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive, and Caddy decides between them by
# PATH MATCHER LENGTH, not by the order they are written in: it sorts routes of
# the same directive longest matcher first, trailing `*` trimmed, ties broken
# lexically (caddyconfig/httpcaddyfile/directives.go, sortRoutes, read at
# v2.11.4, the version this toolkit pins). A `handle` with no path matcher
# sorts last, which is what makes the bare one at the end of this file MAS's
# fallback.
#
# So the three compatibility login paths reach MAS because
# `/_matrix/client/*/login` and its two siblings are longer than `/_matrix/*`,
# and they would still reach MAS if somebody moved them below it. The file is
# written in the order it sorts into so that reading it top to bottom matches
# what happens, but the file's order is a courtesy to the reader and nothing
# more. The proof is in this very file: the `/.well-known/matrix/*` handle
# below is written after both catch alls and still wins, because it is longer.
#
# What would actually break the split is a matcher, not a move: a new handle
# under /_matrix whose path is LONGER than one of the login paths and overlaps
# it would take those requests to wherever it points. Every login then reaches
# the homeserver, which no longer knows how to answer one, and the failure
# reads as a broken client rather than a broken proxy.
#
# Everything outside /_matrix and /_synapse is MAS's own surface: /login,
# /account, /authorize, /upstream/callback/<id> and
# /.well-known/openid-configuration. That is what the final handle is for.
#
# The delegation documents are not here. They belong to whichever hostname
# appears in user identifiers, which is this kind's wellknown role and its own
# snippet.
#
# NEVER GATE THIS HOSTNAME. A Matrix client is not a browser and will not
# follow a redirect to a passkey prompt, so a gate here breaks every client
# login rather than protecting anything. Access control belongs at the identity
# provider, where the group restriction decides who may be provisioned at all.
handle /_matrix/client/*/login {
	reverse_proxy 10.44.0.3:8009 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
	}
}
handle /_matrix/client/*/logout {
	reverse_proxy 10.44.0.3:8009 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
	}
}
handle /_matrix/client/*/refresh {
	reverse_proxy 10.44.0.3:8009 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
	}
}
handle /_matrix/* {
	reverse_proxy 10.44.0.3:8008 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
		# Media and event uploads are larger than a default proxy read allows
		# for.
		transport http {
			read_timeout 5m
		}
	}
}
handle /_synapse/* {
	reverse_proxy 10.44.0.3:8008 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
		# Media and event uploads are larger than a default proxy read allows
		# for.
		transport http {
			read_timeout 5m
		}
	}
}
handle {
	reverse_proxy 10.44.0.3:8009 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
	}
}
